  "thesis": {
    "counterparty": "Leverage-constrained / benchmark-relative funds that cannot simply lever up a low-vol book and instead reach for volatility (and beta) directly, systematically overpaying for high-vol names.",
    "falsifiable_claim": "Stocks in the bottom quintile of trailing 42-day realized volatility outperform the top quintile over the next 5 sessions.",
    "horizon_days": 5,
    "mechanism": "The low-volatility anomaly: leverage-constrained and benchmark-relative investors bid up high-volatility names seeking amplified returns, pushing their risk-adjusted expected return down; low-volatility names are correspondingly under-owned and outperform on a risk-adjusted basis (Ang, Hodrick, Xing & Zhang 2006; classical_low_volatility in src/zoo.py, adapted to a 42-day window for lower turnover).",
    "regime": "calm",
    "why_not_arbitraged": "Exploiting it fully requires leverage most benchmark-relative funds cannot take on capital-efficiently; the edge is real but structurally hard for the natural arbitrageur to scale."
  },
